Webb's main mirror is 6.5 m across, wider than a giraffe is tall. It is made of 18 [[@honeycomb|hexagons]] of a light metal called beryllium, each coated with a very thin layer of gold, which reflects infrared light well. All the gold on the mirror together weighs about as much as a golf ball.

The mirror was too big to fit inside any rocket. So engineers designed Webb to fold up, like [[@origami-maths|origami]], and unfold in space. After launch, it took about two weeks to unfold its sunshield and mirror. Hundreds of parts had to work perfectly, with no one there to fix them.

{{story:honeycomb}}

Diclofenac is safe for cattle at normal doses, and people take it too. But a vulture's body cannot get rid of it. The drug damages the bird's [[@kidneys|kidneys]], and the bird dies within days.

Because vultures feed together, one poisoned carcass could kill many birds at once. Scientists later calculated that less than 1 in 100 carcasses needed to carry the drug to cause a crash this fast. That is how a few treated cows could empty India's skies.

{{story:extinction-today}}

Different animals use light in different ways. The [[@anglerfish|anglerfish]] dangles a glowing lure in front of its mouth, and small fish swim towards it. Some squid and fish have lights along their bellies that match the faint light coming from above. Seen from below, they blend in, as if they had no shadow at all. This trick is called counter-illumination.

Light can also be a weapon. The vampire squid can squirt out a cloud of glowing [[mucus|mucus]] to confuse a [[predator|predator]], then slip away into the dark. Other animals flash brightly when grabbed, a "burglar alarm" that may attract a larger hunter to eat the attacker.

{{story:fireflies}}

Every animal uses electricity. Your own [[@nervous-system|nerves]] send messages as tiny electrical pulses, and your heart beats because of them. Electric eels took this further. Their electrocytes are changed muscle cells that no longer move. Instead, each one pumps charged particles across its walls and then releases them all at once.

Each cell adds about 0.15 volts. Line up thousands, one after another, and the total climbs to hundreds of volts. The current flows out from the head end and back into the tail end through the water, through any fish in the way.

{{story:electricity-made}}

Wombats cannot see well, but they have an excellent sense of smell. They pile their cubes on rocks, logs and the tops of small mounds, often near their burrows. Each pile is a smelly message that says something like "this patch is mine".

Here, shape matters. A round dropping on a sloping rock would roll off. A cube sits still. Many scientists think that's the reason cube poop [[evolve|evolved]], although no one has proved that cubes help wombats find mates or defend their patch better.

{{story:dino-poop}}

लाल, हरा और नीला ही क्यों? इसका जवाब रोशनी में नहीं, तुम्हारी आँखों में है। हर आँख के पिछले हिस्से में रंग पहचानने वाली तीन तरह की [[cell|कोशिकाएँ]] होती हैं, जिन्हें शंकु (कोन) कहते हैं। मोटे तौर पर एक तरह का शंकु सबसे ज़्यादा लाल-सी रोशनी पर जागता है, एक हरी पर और एक नीली पर। तुम्हारा दिमाग़ इनके संकेतों की तुलना करके हर वह रंग पहचानता है जो तुम [[@eyes-see|देखते]] हो।

लाल और हरी रोशनी एक साथ डालो तो "लाल" और "हरे" दोनों शंकु जाग जाते हैं। तुम्हारा दिमाग़ इस मेल को पीला समझता है, जबकि वहाँ पीली रोशनी नहीं है। स्क्रीनें यह तरकीब हर सेकंड लाखों बार लगाती हैं। फ़ोन की स्क्रीन के किसी सफ़ेद हिस्से को पानी की एक बूँद के आर-पार देखो, तो अक्सर अलग-अलग लाल, हरे और नीले बिंदु दिख जाते हैं।

{{story:sky-blue}}

कांगड़ा के चित्रकार अक्सर कविताओं को आधार बनाकर काम करते थे। 12वीं सदी में कवि जयदेव का लिखा गीत गोविंद राधा और कृष्ण के प्रेम, उनकी रूठा-रूठी और फिर से मिलन का वर्णन करता है। चित्रकारों ने हर छंद को एक दृश्य में बदल दिया।

उन्होंने बारहमासा भी चित्रित किया। ये बारह महीनों पर लिखी कविताएँ हैं, जो बताती हैं कि बिछड़े या साथ बैठे प्रेमियों को हर मौसम कैसा लगता है। कांगड़ा चित्रों की स्त्रियाँ बारीक नाक-नक्श, लंबे काले बाल और हल्के से झुकाव के साथ बनाई गई हैं। सुंदरता के इस आदर्श को लोग आज भी 'कांगड़ा चेहरा' कहते हैं।

{{story:monsoon}}

Planes called air tankers swoop low and drop thousands of litres of water or red fire retardant in a single pass. Helicopters carry huge buckets on cables and scoop water from lakes or rivers.

In April 2024, forest fires spread near Nainital in Uttarakhand. Indian Air Force [[@helicopters|helicopters]] filled buckets from Bhimtal lake and dropped the water on burning hillsides. Aircraft rarely put a big fire out alone, though. They slow it down so ground crews can finish the job.

{{story:forest-fires}}

In 1848, about 300 people met in Seneca Falls, New York, and 100 of them signed a declaration that "all men and women are created equal". It took 72 more years, and the 19th Amendment, before American women won the vote nationally in 1920.

[[@new-zealand|New Zealand]] got there first among self-governing countries, in 1893, after women gathered a petition so long it was rolled out across the floor of Parliament. In Britain, campaigners called suffragettes chained themselves to railings and were sent to prison. Some countries were much slower. Swiss women could not vote in national elections until 1971, and one small canton, Appenzell Innerrhoden, kept women out of its local votes until a court forced it to change in 1990. The story of [[@womens-vote|how women won the vote]] is full of people who never lived to see it.

{{story:womens-vote}}

At first, actors had to sing for themselves, live on set, with musicians sometimes hidden behind scenery out of camera range. That meant actors were often hired for their voices, not their acting.

In 1935, filmmakers in Calcutta began recording songs separately and having actors mime to them. This was playback [[@singing|singing]], and it changed everything. Now the best singers could sing for any actor. Singers such as [[@lata-mangeshkar|Lata Mangeshkar]], Mohammed Rafi, Kishore Kumar and Asha Bhosle sang thousands of songs. In 2011, Guinness [[@world-records|World Records]] named Asha Bhosle the most recorded artist in music history.

{{story:record-music}}

Four awards are open to every kind of music and are considered the biggest. Album of the Year goes to a whole album. Record of the Year goes to one recorded song, including how it was performed and produced. Song of the Year goes to the songwriters. Best New Artist goes to a newcomer.

[[@taylor-swift|Taylor Swift]] became the first artist to win Album of the Year four times, in 2024. Beyoncé has won more Grammys than anyone else, 35 by the 2025 ceremony, when she also won Album of the Year for the first time.

{{story:ravi-shankar}}

Public voting turns viewers into part of the show. It also means the best singer doesn't always win. A contestant with a strong home region or a moving story can collect huge numbers of votes.

In Indian Idol's third season in 2007, Prashant Tamang from Darjeeling won. People in the hills of North Bengal, [[@sikkim|Sikkim]] and Nepal voted for him in huge numbers. Counting votes fairly is a hard problem, much like counting them in a real [[@vote-how|election]].

{{story:vote-how}}

After World War II, [[@plastic-made|plastic]] was new and exciting. In 1947 LEGO bought a plastic moulding machine, and it came with samples of Kiddicraft bricks. Two years later, LEGO made its own version, called Automatic Binding Bricks.

They didn't sell well at first. The bricks didn't grip very tightly, and towers fell over. Many shops sent them back, preferring wood and metal toys.

{{story:minecraft-coding}}

बाल [[keratin|केराटिन]] नाम के एक सख़्त पदार्थ से बने हैं, जिससे तुम्हारे नाखून भी बने हैं। बाल का जो हिस्सा दिखता है, वह ज़िंदा नहीं है। सारी हलचल नीचे जड़ में होती है, जहाँ नई कोशिकाएँ बनती हैं और ऊपर धकेली जाती हैं, और रास्ते में मेलानिन इकट्ठा करती जाती हैं।

यानी बाल का रंग उसके बनते समय ही तय हो जाता है। सिर के बाल लगभग 1 सेंटीमीटर हर महीने बढ़ते हैं, इसलिए जो बाल आज सफ़ेद उग रहा है, उसकी पूरी लंबाई में सफ़ेदी दिखने में महीनों लगेंगे। इसीलिए सफ़ेदी आम तौर पर पहले जड़ों पर दिखती है, और इसीलिए रातों-रात बाल सफ़ेद होने की कहानियाँ शायद बढ़ा-चढ़ाकर सुनाई जाती हैं।

{{story:genes-family}}

By 1990, India was borrowing heavily abroad. Then the Gulf War sent oil prices soaring and cut off money sent home by Indians working in Kuwait and Iraq. Political trouble at home made foreign lenders nervous.

By June 1991, India's foreign reserves could pay for only about three weeks of imports. To get emergency loans, the government pledged gold. About 47 tonnes from the Reserve Bank of India were flown to London and pledged with the Bank of England and the Bank of Japan. It felt like a family pawning its jewellery, and many Indians were shocked.

{{story:world-bank-imf}}

In December 1892, a young monk named Swami Vivekananda reached Kanyakumari after travelling across India. He swam out to a rock about 500 metres from the shore and meditated there for three days, thinking about the country's poverty and its future. The next year, he sailed to the USA and spoke at the World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago, where he became famous.

In 1970, the Vivekananda Rock Memorial opened on that rock. Ferries carry visitors across. A stone footprint there is believed by devotees to be the goddess Kanya Kumari's.

{{story:thirukkural}}

A temple has stood in Madurai for a very long time. Ancient Tamil poems from the [[@tamil-sangam|Sangam]] era mention Madurai as a great city.

In the early 1300s, the temple was damaged during raids by armies from the north. Later, Madurai came under the rule of the Nayaks, who began as governors for the [[@vijayanagara|Vijayanagara]] Empire. The Nayak kings, especially Thirumalai Nayak in the 1600s, rebuilt and greatly expanded the temple. Most of what visitors see today dates from the 1500s and 1600s.

{{story:architecture-temples}}

Indonesia's islands are full of wildlife found nowhere else. Komodo dragons, the world's largest lizards, live on Komodo and a few nearby islands. They can grow about 3 m long, nearly as long as a small car. They hunt deer and wild pigs, and their [[saliva|saliva]] carries [[venom|venom]] that stops blood from clotting. Only a few thousand are left in the wild.

The rainforests of Sumatra and Borneo hold orangutans and the Rafflesia, which has the largest single flower in the world, nearly 1 m across. It smells of rotting meat, which attracts the flies that pollinate it.

{{story:srivijaya}}
